  "notes": "{掃除|そうじ} refers to cleaning a space by sweeping, wiping, vacuuming, or tidying up. It covers general cleaning rather than specialized tasks like laundry or dishwashing.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- {掃除|そうじ}する / {掃除|そうじ}をする: to clean\n- {掃除機|そうじき}をかける: to vacuum\n- {掃除|そうじ}が{行|い}き{届|とど}いている: well-maintained, kept clean\n\nTYPES OF CLEANING:\n- {大掃除|おおそうじ}: major year-end cleaning (a Japanese tradition before the New Year)\n- {部屋|へや}の{掃除|そうじ}: room cleaning\n- トイレ{掃除|そうじ}: toilet cleaning\n- {掃除|そうじ}{当番|とうばん}: cleaning duty (common in schools and workplaces)\n\nCULTURAL NOTE:\nIn Japanese schools, students clean their own classrooms and common areas daily ({掃除|そうじ}の{時間|じかん}). {大掃除|おおそうじ} in late December is a widespread tradition of thoroughly cleaning the home before the New Year.",
